-- Platformer — gravity, a jump, and standing on things.
--
-- What this exercises that the ball games do not:
--   * acceleration. `speed` is constant, but a level rule that nudges
--     `ydirection` every tick *is* gravity, and `min` caps the terminal fall.
--   * an `if` guard reading state the collision phase wrote earlier in the same
--     tick, which is what makes a jump need ground under it
--   * an object resting on another rather than bouncing off it
--   * a collectible that disappears and scores

start title

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- title -----
--
-- The artwork is demade by the image engine at the screen's own size, into a
-- tilemap the background layer draws for nothing — the same fitter a photograph
-- goes through. What it costs is tiles, and the compiler says so when a picture
-- wants more than the game's own art has left.

scene title

backdrop platformer.title.svg

create text prompt in title (x centerx - 7, y screenheight - 2, text "press a to play")

when a pressed in title then scene as play


scene play

-- The playfield is demade too, at the screen's own size, by the same fitter the
-- title screen went through. It is scenery and nothing else: the game reads
-- nothing from it and collides with none of it — what it costs is tiles.
backdrop platformer.play.svg

-- A jump fires on the same rule that performs it, so the sound and the impulse
-- cannot come apart: `sound ... on a pressed if footing.value > 0` is the jump
-- rule's own trigger, guard included.
music meadow.mid in play
sound jump.wav on a pressed in play if footing.value > 0
sound coin.wav on player hits coin


create object hero (width 1 cell, height 2 cells, speed 55vw, sprite hero.svg)
create object ledge (width 25vw, height 1 cell, sprite ledge.svg)
create object coin (width 1 cell, height 1 cell, sprite coin.svg)

create hero player in play (x 15vw, y 2)

-- A full-width floor would be a wall of hardware sprites (20 of a Game Boy's 40
-- for the floor alone), and the compiler says so. Static scenery really wants a
-- background layer, which the language does not have yet — so for now the floor
-- is a ledge you can fall off, and `screenbottom` catches you.
create ledge floor in play (x 10vw, y screenheight - 1, width 55vw)
create ledge shelf1 in play (x 35vw, y screenheight - 5)
create ledge shelf2 in play (x 55vw, y screenheight - 9)

-- The shelves step to the right, each overlapping the one below by a cell or
-- two, because a ledge is solid from underneath as well as on top — the
-- separation says so. A shelf directly overhead is a ceiling, not a platform:
-- the way up is to jump *beside* the next one and step across. Nothing overhangs
-- where the hero starts, either, or it would begin the game on a shelf.
create coin coin1 in play (x 25vw, y screenheight - 2)
create coin coin2 in play (x 45vw, y screenheight - 6)
create coin coin3 in play (x 65vw, y screenheight - 10)

-- Whether the hero has something under its feet *right now*. A `number` with
-- `visible 0` is not drawn, not collided with and not moved, which makes it the
-- one place a game can keep a plain value.
--
-- The three rules that touch it read in tick order, and that is the whole trick:
-- it is cleared with the level rules, set by the collision phase when something
-- was landed on, and read by the edge-triggered jump after both. So it is true
-- for exactly the ticks the hero is standing on something.
create number footing in play (value 0, visible 0)

create text label in play (x 1, y 1, text "coins:")
create number score in play (value 0, x 8, y 1)

-- Gravity: a constant downward nudge, clamped so the fall has a terminal speed.
-- Without the clamp a long drop tunnels through the floor in one tick, which the
-- compiler would tell you about.
when always in play then player.ydirection as min(player.ydirection + 0.04, 0.9)
when always in play then footing.value as 0

control player left (xdirection -1) on hold
control player right (xdirection 1) on hold

when player hits screenleft, screenright then xdirection as 0
when player hits screentop then ydirection as 0
when player touches screenbottom then (ydirection, footing.value) as (0, 1)

-- Landing, and bonking your head, in one rule.
--
-- This is `touches`, not `hits`, and the difference is the whole reason
-- `touches` exists: `hits` fires once, on contact, so gravity would go on adding
-- to `ydirection` every tick the hero stood still. The separation would keep it
-- looking correct while the state ran away underneath — and the first jump after
-- a long stand would be fought by a full tick of accumulated fall.
--
-- The guard splits the two ways a hero meets a ledge. Coming down onto it
-- (`>= 0`) is a landing: the fall stops and there is footing. Coming up into it
-- is a bonk: the climb stops and nothing is stood on, so a jump cannot be
-- renewed by clipping the underside of a shelf.
when player touches ledge if player.ydirection >= 0 then (ydirection, footing.value) as (0, 1) else ydirection as 0

-- The jump. It is an edge-triggered rule rather than a `control` for one reason:
-- a `control` runs at the top of the tick, before anything has been collided
-- with, so it cannot ask whether there is ground underfoot — and a jump that
-- cannot ask is a jump you can press forever, climbing the sky a press at a
-- time.
--
-- -1.5 against 0.04 of gravity is a jump about five cells high and a second and
-- a quarter long, which clears the four-cell step between shelves with a little
-- to spare. Both numbers are multiples of the hero's own `speed`, so the arc
-- scales with the playfield exactly as its running does.
when a pressed if footing.value > 0 then player.ydirection as -1.5

when player hits coin then (coin.visible, score.value) as (0, score.value + 1)
